  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSaturday Night/Sunday: Confidence is high that the ridge will\nbreak down by Saturday night with shortwave energy moving down\ninto the area from the northwest. A low level jet looks to set\nup a little farther east than previous nights and will allow \nfor an area of scattered showers and storms to form in Kansas \nand slide southeast into the area overnight into Sunday morning.\nThe latest HREF shows mean MU CAPE approaching 1000j/kg along \nand west of Highway 65 after 10pm with the 1-hr thunder probs at\n30-60% in this area after midnight, lasting to sunrise Sunday. \nThis will linger into Sunday morning before decreasing. This \nappears to be our highest rain chances of the weekend, however \nits mainly for locations along and west of Highway 65. Mean \nrainfall amounts are less than 0.50inch. Currently the NBM has \nhigh temps on Sunday in the low 70s across the Rolla area to the\nlower 80s across the rest of the area. It is possible that this\ngradient will be sharper depending on how long morning \nclouds/rain holds on and temp adjustments made need to be made.\n\nMid Week System: Ensembles continue to suggest\nthat a stronger system will move into the desert southwest and \neventually the central plains during the Tuesday-Thursday time \nframe next week. Confidence is high in the rain chances \n(currently 50-70%) however significant structural and timing \ndifferences in the trough are still evident (either a \nprogressive/amplified trough or a closed off low). Alot of this \nis due to the uncertainty of the remnants of Hurricane Polo off\nthe coast of Mexico. Guidance has this curving northeast into \nthe Baja and then getting absorbed into our system. If this does\noccur then it would increase the amount of moisture available \nwith the system and lead to several rounds of rainfall. The \ntrend over the last 24 hours has been for a slower arrival of \nthis system with the higher rain chances delayed more towards \nWednesday. Latest CPC/WPC outlooks does indicate the potential \nfor heavy/excessive rainfall with this system which would be \nwelcome given the ongoing/worsening drought over a large portion\nof the area.",
